00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:06.789 align:start position:0%
 
okay so batman is a character that i've  always been fascinated with i've  collected hundreds of graphic novels for  him platinumed all of the arkham games  several times and poured over every film  and tv show starring the dark knight  that i could find ever since i was a kid  what i've always loved about batman is  that he's someone who can be mulder to  fit almost any message that you want to  put across but at the heart of him there  still remains the fundamental belief  that justice must be upheld whether it's  the campness of the 1966 version the  darker more graphic timber intakes lego  batman the animated series batman v  superman or the grounded nolan films  every iteration of him feels like it's  valid i'm kind of ashamed to admit it  but when robert pattinson was first  announced as playing the character i  kind of shook my head and thought like  many that we'd be getting a  twilight-esque take aimed at teens just  a few years prior dc had massively  changed zack snyder's vision into  becoming the abomination that was  justice league and i wrongly assume that  they'd taken ben affleck out the role to  make things more like the mcu however  every single trailer every single tv  spot has made me realize that i couldn't  have been more wrong and the upcoming  batman film in my eyes has the potential  to be one of the best comic book movies  ever made it can be cruel poetic or  blind  but when it's denied  it's your violence you may  find in every interview that matt reeves  gives he just seems to get it and when  looking on liner comics the other day i  came across the collection of graphic  novels that is releasing alongside the  film this box set includes batman year  one the long halloween and also ego the  first of these tells the story of james  gordon transferring from gotham whilst  bruce wayne arrives home after his years  training abroad together the pair learn  to put their differences to the side and  come the end of it they form an alliance  that will help to take down corruption  in the city the long halloween is one of  my favorite graphic novels of all time  and this murder mystery focuses on  batman stopping a mysterious killer  called holiday from carrying out attacks  on the mob the book very much inspired  the dark knight and this year 2 story  feels like the closest thing we'll ever  get to the godfather being adapted into  a superhero story lastly his ego  something that i was surprised to see in  the lineup but after thinking about it  it makes a lot of sense the darwin cook  story follows batman failing to stop one  of the joker's henchmen from ending his  own life instead of facing up to his  boss racked with guilt he returns home  to the cave and bruce and the batman's  psyche split down the middle and they  both go back and forth over what it  means to be batman and what the future  should hold bruce very much has to  battle it out with his toughest enemy  yet his own ego and through this  self-exploration he learns what it means  to be batman it's a story that has  always fascinated me and when looking at  all the trailers and teasers i think one  thing becomes clear this is a batman  story  who's swing  sorry now before you start calling me  captain obvious yet what i mean by that  is that it's very apparent that the  batman is the true face whereas bruce is  the mask every time we see him in his  civilian clothes bruce barely talks  interacts or even seems interested in  what's going on during the funeral scene  that was posted online he barely says a  word and is clearly only attending the  event because he wants to learn  information that will help out batman  this is something that we have seen  explored in other films like batman  begins but bruce was always put forward  as the more dominant personality and  even the playboy one had a footing in  there too however here bruce wayne just  seems like an afterthought and it very  much reminds me of books like batman  zero yeah in that bruce returned to  gotham and he immediately started  patrolling the knight locking himself  away in the cave through the day he had  no interest in being bruce wayne and it  was only after the first talk that  alfred made him even consider bringing  that side of his life into the light  that's very much the feeling that i get  from the matt reeves movie and the title  of batman seems so fitting because  that's all that bruce appears to care  about here is a mentally damaged man  filled with trauma who will do whatever  he can to make sure no one has to  experience what he went through he's  completely obsessed with being batman  and i think the film will very much  explore the course that infatuation has  on someone's life now on the other side  of this is the riddler and opposite to  batman the riddler  is asking for you  the killer left this for the batman why  is he writing to you  is trying to keep everything in order  the riddler is someone who will strike  the match to burn down everything just  so that he can rebuild from the ashes in  the film he will be attempting to take  down the high ranking members of society  because he was clearly affected by them  at some point in its life it very much  feels like something that was ripped out  of the man who laughs which featured the  joker he killed several high-ranking  citizens in gotham and even targeted  bruce wayne which we know from the  trailers is something that the riddler  will also be doing all of these pieces  come together to show that matt reeves  is pulling some amazing elements from  the comics and it demonstrates that he  fully understands the source material  what he's also done though is to create  something that doesn't necessarily feel  like it's completely based on any  specific element and everything from the  batsuit to the batmobile feels unique  and innovative one comic i haven't  mentioned so far as batman year 100  which i also think that you should  definitely pick up if you haven't set in  the future this batman fully immerses  himself in the role and even puts fake  vampire teeth in his mouth just so that  he can get into the minds of criminals  there's definitely a darkness to the  character and what reeves has done is  that he's translated this to every  single frame in his film cinematography  is something that is often said to be  lacking in comic book movies and a lot  of the time this is purely down to the  process in which these films are made  shot heavily on green screens or even  completely computer generated it's  difficult for directors to capture a  true look and vision for their films  however reeves movie for the most part  was shot on location either in glasgow  or on sets and the reason that we have  so many behind the scenes sucks on the  film already is because it was possible  for the public to go down and see what  was being shot this adds an authenticity  to the movie and it allows the director  to see what he's filming as he films it  he isn't sending this off to vfx artists  with a description of what you go in the  background he's capturing it himself and  because of this his movie looks  completely different from anything  that's come before i'm struggling to  think of a comic book movie that looks  as good as the batman does and it's  definitely got its own style that you  can instantly see from any imager  trailer if you placed distilled a frame  of it down with another movie from the  genre and asked someone to point out  which one was the batman i think they'd  instantly be able to tell because of  reeves ability to craft a unique style  this extends to his costumes and one  thing that always bugged me about the  mcu was the reliance on cgi once tom  holland often complains in interviews  how his spider-man doesn't have a place  where he can pee but when you look at  his films his costume is often cgi'd for  the final edit this is extremely  noticeable now in movies like civil war  and after seeing how good garfield's one  looked in the amazing spider-man it  makes you wonder why marvel have started  to go down this route the batsuit though  looks real and that's because it is this  feels handcrafted and the cowl that's  stitched together is somewhat  reminiscent of the one from gotham by  gaslight the armor feels like it was  ripped right out of an arkham game and  all of it has a practicality to it  that's difficult not to appreciate even  with half the budget what most comic  book movies get reeves has elevated  things to a level where it looks better  than most in the genre and he's really  shown why creatives should always be put  above corporations in a time where a lot  of comic book movies feel by the numbers  he's taking his own vision and style to  a level that will make this movie stand  out from the pack batman is a character  that has been done to death at this  point but somehow reeves and many others  constantly find new ways to reinvent him  and bring him back as someone who can  still be modern while standing for his  traditional values the action looks  incredible too and just in the same way  that zack snyder completely nailed the  warehouse scene in bbs the fight in the  smoke against the riddler's goons feels  like the perfect way to showcase batman  on film this is someone who's mastered  several martial arts and gadgets and  watching him dispatch people with ease  will never not be satisfying  [Music]  there's also the detective elements to  this film as well and some moments of it  feel like they were ripped right out of  silence of the lamps we know from tv  spots that bruce will also be using  special contact lenses to record crime  scenes and reeves has said that he's  really pushing this film so that we can  finally see batman as the renowned  world's greatest detective it's  something that we haven't had before in  live action and when going against  cerebral villains like the riddler  batman will need to be operating at that  level too now beyond this we also have  the world building in the movie and  reeves has managed to bring in  characters like catwoman the penguin  carmine falcone and other mainstays of  the universe in a way that makes it  flesh out gotham this is the city that's  so corrupt that the lines are often  blurred between the criminals and the  upstanding citizens and we see in zoe  kravitz's catwoman how people have to  walk the line in order to survive it's  something i'm fascinated with in film  and often the villains are the ones that  we relate to the most because we realize  just how easy it is to slip into  darkness that's what seems to be the  best aspect of the batman and i can't  wait to see if he too wrestles with  doing the right thing when he uncovers  just how corrupt that the city is from  the trailers it looks like even the  wanes will be pulled into it and exposed  for what they've done to a massive  fortune i think that the film is really  gonna put several moral quandaries in  place for batman to try and navigate  overall it seems like it's gonna be  incredible and i hope that you enjoyed  us discussing why we're so excited for  it obviously i'd love to hear your  thoughts on the film below and if you're  hyped for it or not if you want  something else to watch then make sure  you check out our breakdown of the third  trailer for the batman which will be  linked on screen right now we went over  all the easter eggs we could find in it  pointing out some of the plot details  that we've unearthed and it's definitely  worth checking out if you're interested  in the film if not then thanks for  sticking through the video i've been  poor i'll see you on the next one take  care peace  you

